<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan was a semi-legendary Himyarite king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, remembered in Arabic folk tradition. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayfawa-dynasty">Sayfawa dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kanem">Kanem</a> claimed descent from him, and the dynasty took its name from his. The consolidation of power in Kanem is traditionally associated with him, although historically verifiable rulers came later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sayf-ibn-dhi-yazan.jpg" alt="Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan" width="272" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ahmed'Aliaan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ali_Bey-_Sultan_Saif-Zuliazan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The Arabic sources disagree on his real name: al-Hamdani gives Shurahbil ibn 'Amr, nicknamed Saif for his courage, Ibn Hisham gives Ma'dikarib ibn Abi Murrah al-Fayyad, and Tabari records both views and a third, Saifan ibn Ma'dikarib. Dhi Yazan refers to the Dhu Yazan, an elite ruling family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom</a> that had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> as early as the late 4th century. Saif was born around 516 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanaa">Sana'a</a>. Abraha later exiled his father from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> in order to marry his mother, Rayhana bint Dhi Jadan, and their son Masruq ibn Abraha was Saif's half-brother.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Saif sought to remove Masruq from the throne of Himyar, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> refused to help, as it shared Masruq's religion. He turned instead to the Lakhmid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-numan-iii-ibn-al-mundhir">Al-Nu'man III ibn al-Mundhir</a>, who brought him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a>, whose kinship Saif claimed by pointing to his white skin against the dark skin of the Abyssinian people. Khosrow agreed on condition that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> become a Sasanian vassal and sent the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wahrez">Wahrez</a> with him; Tabari reports that at least eight ships sailed, two of which sank. The account holds a chronological error, since Al-Nu'man III began his reign around 580–583, more than ten years after Khosrow's death.</p><p>Once Masruq had been killed in battle, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> placed Saif on the throne of Himyar as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> paying yearly tribute. A tradition tells that a delegation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> led by the elderly Abdul Muttalib visited him and that Saif told him his grandson, the future prophet Muhammad, would one day receive prophecy. The historian Ignác Goldziher denied that any such meeting took place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Saif was stabbed to death by one of his Abyssinian servants between 575 and 578. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wahrez">Wahrez</a> returned with Sasanian troops and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, beginning the period of Sasanian Yemen. The Encyclopedia Iranica names a probable son, Ma'dikarib, as his successor, while Safiur Rahman Mubarakpuri held that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> allowed no Yemeni rule at all. The Sīrat Sayf ibn Dhī-Yazan, a romance attributed to him, blends history with fiction and takes him into the realm of the jinn. The Yemeni revolutionary Muhammad Mahmoud Al-Zubairi drew on his memory, and a Jordanian television series of 1982 told his story.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saif ibn Dhi Yazan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Kanem–Bornu Empire began around the ninth century north-east of Lake Chad as the Kingdom of Kanem, ruled first by the Duguwa dynasty, linked to the Zaghawa, and from the late eleventh century by the Sayfawa, who claimed descent from the Yemeni hero of legend <b>Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Himyarite noble <b>Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan</b> sought help from Persia, and around 570 a Sasanian force under Wahriz defeated the Ethiopians, opening the period of Sasanian Yemen.</blockquote><a href="/state/aksumite-yemen">Aksumite Yemen</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 570 the Sasanian king Khosrow I sent a small force under the general Wahriz at the request of <b>Sayf ibn Dhi Yazan</b> to expel the Ethiopians of Aksumite Yemen.</blockquote><a href="/state/sasanian-yemen">Sasanian Yemen</a></li></ul></section>
